# Ignore files and directories starting with a dot followed by uppercase letters
.[A-Z]*

# Standard Go ignores
bin/
obj/
*.exe
*.exe~
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib
*.test
*.out
# Anchored to the module root, deliberately. An unanchored "vendor/" matches
# a directory of that name at any depth, which silently excluded
# internal/ui/static/vendor -- the third-party JS the controller embeds and
# redistributes. git add reports nothing when it skips an ignored path, so
# the commit that claimed to add those assets did not, and the committed
# tree failed to build on //go:embed with "pattern vendor: no matching
# files found". Go's vendor directory only ever exists at the module root,
# so the leading slash costs nothing and is what the pattern always meant.
/vendor/
*.log

# Crawl-tier per-project secrets (internal/credential): the AES-256-GCM
# master key and the credentials file it encrypts. Never commit either,
# even though the credentials file itself holds only ciphertext.
.pleiades/

# The compose stack's secrets, which `make up` and `make setup` write: the
# master encryption key that reads every credential the stack stores, and the
# JWT secret. It is the only copy of the key, so it is backed up by hand and
# never committed. Anchored to the module root, where docker compose reads it.
/.env

# The throwaway serving certificate `make dev-cert` writes. It holds a real
# private key, so it is ignored even though the key is worthless outside a
# development machine: a committed key teaches the habit of committing keys,
# and the file is one command to regenerate. Anchored to the module root,
# like /vendor/ above, because that is the one place the make target names.
/.dev-certs/

# The certificate the controller provisions for ITSELF when no real one is
# configured. Its directory defaults to a relative "tls", resolved against
# the working directory, which is /data inside the container and this
# checkout when somebody runs ./controller from here. Same reasoning as
# above and the same reasoning as the scratch databases below: a real
# private key that a run leaves behind must not be committable.
/tls/

# Manually built binaries from local `go build -o <name> ./cmd/<name>`
# verification runs (each cmd/ subdirectory's own name). Not *.exe-style
# extensions, so the Standard Go ignores above miss them.
/controller
/runner
/pleiades
/docs-lint
/gendocs
/uidev
/testgate
/sweep
# Scratch databases. cmd/controller defaults DB_PATH to a SQLite file beside
# the binary, and a DSN handed to it on the command line is resolved relative
# to the working directory, so running it from a checkout leaves one here. One
# was committed this way before this rule existed.
*.db
*.db-shm
*.db-wal
